<!-- Not a v7 page: v7 had no renderer for this dialect and no dialect to render.  See BESM-6 NOTES. -->

# MANVIEW(1)

## NAME

manview - format a manual page

## SYNOPSIS

| **manview** [ **-p** ] [ **-m** ] [ **-w** *cols* ] [ *file* ... ]

## DESCRIPTION

*Manview* reads a manual page written in Unix Manual Markdown and writes it formatted for a
terminal. It is what man(1) runs over a page it has found, and it is the only formatter on
this system: there is no nroff(1) here and none is coming.

With no *file* argument, or with a *file* named `-`, the page is read from the standard
input. More than one *file* may be given; each is formatted in turn with a blank line
between, which is what **man -a** asks for.

A paragraph is filled to the output width and a word is never hyphenated. A line block, a
fenced block and a heading keep the line structure the source gave them. A definition, a
list item and a quote are indented five columns, which is v7's step; a definition whose tag
fits that step keeps its body on the tag's line. A literal run is quoted, the quotes being
*manview*\&'s and not the source's, and a cross-reference is set with its name in one font
and its parentheses in roman.

The level-1 title becomes a page banner: the page's own name at both margins with the
manual's name between them. There is no footer, since more(1) does the paging here and
there is no page number to put in one.

**-p**
: Plain. No escape sequence of any kind is written, so the output is legible in a file and
  may be compared byte for byte. Quotation marks and the parentheses of a cross-reference
  are punctuation and stay.

**-m**
: Monochrome. Bold, italic and underline are written, colour is not. For a terminal that
  understands the one and not the other.

**-w** *cols*
: Set the output width, 80 by default. A value below 20 is raised to 20 and one above 512
  is lowered to it. There is no way to ask a terminal how wide it is on this system, which
  is why this option exists.

An argument that is not an option ends the option list, and so does `--`. A page that
cannot be opened is reported and the rest are formatted anyway.

## THE FORMAT

Unix Manual Markdown is a small semantic dialect of Markdown, described in this tree's
`doc/Manual_Page_Format.md`, and *manview* implements it and nothing else. There is no link
syntax, no raw HTML, no setext heading and no thematic break, and a construct this program
does not recognize is text rather than an extension.

Four span kinds are recognized: roman, `**bold**` for what is typed, `*italic*` for what is
replaced, and a backquoted run for a token quoted as itself. An underscore is never a
delimiter, so `time_t` and `SIG_DFL` need no escaping. A `name(N)` written with no markup at
all is a cross-reference. Nine block kinds are recognized: paragraph, line block, fenced
block, definition list, bullet list, ordered list, quote, comment and heading.

## FILES

| `/usr/man/man?/*` the pages this formats
| `/usr/bin/man` what finds one and runs this over it
| `/bin/more` what man pipes the result into

## SEE ALSO

man(1), more(1), col(1)

## DIAGNOSTICS

The exit status is 1 if any *file* could not be opened, and 0 otherwise.

A page holding a single block longer than eight thousand bytes draws `block too long,
output truncated` and is formatted as far as it was read. No page in this manual comes
within half of that.

## BESM-6 NOTES

*Note:* colour and attributes are **on by default and are not conditioned on the output
being a terminal**, which is the opposite of the modern habit and is forced by how man(1)
invokes this program: the display command is `manview page | more`, so *manview*\&'s
standard output is a pipe exactly when a person is reading. **-p** is the plain form and is
what a script or a pipeline wants; **man -** shows the page source itself and formats
nothing.

*Note:* the escape sequences are ANSI, hard-coded, as more(1) and novi(1) already write.
There is no terminal database on this image and no `/usr/lib/more.help`-style data file for
this program either; a terminal that is not ANSI wants **-p**.

*Note:* a column is a **character** and not a byte. This system carries eight bits
everywhere, so a page may hold Cyrillic - seventeen in this manual do - and a paragraph of
it is filled by the letters it has rather than by the bytes they take.

*Note:* backspace overstrike, which the format calls a renderer's default and its fallback,
is **not** implemented. That is nroff's convention and col(1) exists to undo it; nothing on
this machine produces it and nothing but col(1) would read it. **-p** is the fallback here.

*Note:* one block of the page is held at a time and not the page, which is what lets a
manual of any size be read in 28,672 words of address space. The largest block in this
manual is about four thousand bytes and the buffer is eight thousand.

## AUTHOR

Written for this port. The dialect it reads, and the reader it was written from, are
`cmd/man2umm` in the source tree.
